   "l": "Not showing himself, therefore bright;\nnot taking himself as right, therefore standing out;\nnot vaunting himself, therefore having merit;\nnot making much of himself, therefore lasting.\nIt is only because he does not contend\nthat no one under heaven can contend with him.\nWhat antiquity called 'bent, then whole' —\nhow could that be empty words!\nTruly kept whole, and he returns to it.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "He makes no show of himself, and so is seen clearly; does not insist he is right, and so stands out; does not vaunt himself, and so has merit to his name; does not make much of himself, and so lasts. It is precisely because he will not contend that no one under heaven can contend with him. 'Bent, then whole' — the old saying is no empty phrase. In truth it keeps a man whole, and he goes home to the Way by it.",
   "n": "自 always stands in front of its verb, and in these four lines it is the verb's object: 自見 'show himself', 自伐 'vaunt himself'. English has to put the pronoun after. 見 is read xiàn here, 'to show', not jiàn 'to see' — the same character served for 'display' in chapter 19's 見素抱樸. Some read it jiàn, 'does not look at himself', which pairs neatly with 明 'clear-sighted'; but the other three verbs are all self-promotion, and the series decides it. 夫唯 A 故 B, 'it is only because A that B', is the formula from chapters 8 and 15. 莫 needs no subject of its own: it means 'there is no one who'. Then 所謂 … 者 wraps eight characters into a single noun, and 豈 … 哉 makes an outcry of what is grammatically a question. The last five characters are the hardest in the chapter and have no stated subject: 誠全而歸之 can be 'in truth it keeps one whole, and returns him to it (the Way)', which is how we take it, or 'wholeness in earnest, and it comes home to him'. Chinese does not choose, and neither reading can be ruled out."
